<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <title>ipthing traffic analysis</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Aggregated traffic analysis for ipthing.net — who hits a public IP echo service." />
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0f1419" />
  <meta name="application-name" content="ipthing stats" />
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-title" content="ipthing stats" />
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
  <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent" />
  <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
  <link rel="icon" href="favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
  <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" sizes="any" />
  <link rel="icon" href="favicon-32x32.png" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" />
  <link rel="icon" href="favicon-16x16.png" type="image/png" sizes="16x16" />
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="apple-touch-icon.png" sizes="180x180" />
  <link rel="manifest" href="site.webmanifest" />
  <meta property="og:title" content="ipthing traffic analysis" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Aggregated traffic analysis for ipthing.net — who hits a public IP echo service." />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://stats.ipthing.net/" />
  <meta property="og:image" content="https://stats.ipthing.net/android-chrome-512x512.png" />
  <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="ipthing traffic analysis" />
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="Aggregated traffic analysis for ipthing.net." />
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://stats.ipthing.net/android-chrome-512x512.png" />
  <script src="https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-2.35.2.min.js"></script>
  <style>
    :root {
      --bg: #0f1419;
      --panel: #1a222c;
      --text: #e7ecf1;
      --muted: #9aa7b5;
      --accent: #3d9cf0;
      --rule: #2a3542;
    }
    * { box-sizing: border-box; }
    html { -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }
    body {
      margin: 0;
      font-family: "IBM Plex Sans", "Segoe UI", system-ui, sans-serif;
      background: radial-gradient(1200px 600px at 10% -10%, #1a2a3a, var(--bg));
      color: var(--text);
      line-height: 1.55;
      overflow-x: hidden;
    }
    main {
      max-width: 960px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem 4rem;
      width: 100%;
    }
    h1, h2, h3 { line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 600; overflow-wrap: balance; }
    h1 { font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 4.5vw, 2rem); margin-bottom: 0.35rem; }
    h2 {
      margin-top: 2.5rem;
      padding-top: 1rem;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 3.5vw, 1.4rem);
    }
    p.lead { color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.05rem; max-width: 42rem; }
    .meta { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .cards {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr));
      gap: 0.75rem;
      margin: 1.25rem 0 1.75rem;
    }
    .card {
      background: var(--panel);
      border: 1px solid var(--rule);
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .card .label { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
    .card .value {
      font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 3.5vw, 1.35rem);
      margin-top: 0.25rem;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .chart {
      background:
        linear-gradient(145deg, rgba(61, 156, 240, 0.12), transparent 42%),
        linear-gradient(325deg, rgba(86, 207, 225, 0.08), transparent 40%),
        var(--panel);
      border: 1px solid var(--rule);
      border-radius: 14px;
      padding: 0.65rem 0.35rem 0.35rem;
      margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
      overflow: hidden;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 100%;
      box-shadow:
        inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04),
        0 12px 28px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28);
    }
    .chart .js-plotly-plot,
    .chart .plotly-graph-div {
      width: 100% !important;
      max-width: 100%;
    }
    .chart-desktop-only { display: block; }
    .chart-mobile-only { display: none; }
    .table-wrap {
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 100%;
      overflow-x: auto;
      -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
      margin: 0.75rem 0 1.5rem;
      border-radius: 8px;
    }
    table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      margin: 0;
      min-width: 280px;
    }
    th, td {
      text-align: left;
      padding: 0.45rem 0.5rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      vertical-align: top;
    }
    th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 500; font-size: 0.85rem; white-space: nowrap; }
    td { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    td code {
      font-size: 0.85em;
      word-break: break-word;
    }
    .note {
      background: #15202b;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
      padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 1rem 0;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
    li { margin: 0.35rem 0; }
    a { color: var(--accent); }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      main { padding: 1.25rem 0.85rem 3rem; }
      .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0.55rem; }
      .card { padding: 0.7rem 0.75rem; }
      .chart { border-radius: 10px; padding: 0.4rem 0.15rem 0.2rem; }
      .chart-desktop-only { display: none; }
      .chart-mobile-only { display: block; }
      th, td { padding: 0.4rem 0.4rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
      p.lead { font-size: 0.98rem; }
    }

    @media (max-width: 380px) {
      .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>What hits a public IP echo service?</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Analysis of request logs from <a href="https://ipthing.net">ipthing.net</a> —
    a small service that shows clients their IP, headers, and TLS details.
    This is not a census of the internet; it is a long-running sample of who
    probes (or uses) such an endpoint.
  </p>
  <p class="meta">Report generated {{ generated_at }}</p>

  <h2>Dataset at a glance</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Requests</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(overview.requests) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Unique IPs</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(overview.unique_ips) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">First day</div><div class="value">{{ overview.first_day }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Last day</div><div class="value">{{ overview.last_day }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">HTTPS</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(overview.https_requests) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">HTTP</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(overview.http_requests) }}</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    Until Wednesday 23&nbsp;September&nbsp;2026 the service only allowed
    <code>GET</code>, so historical method mix is almost entirely
    <code>GET /</code>. Scanners still leave fingerprints in query-parameter
    <em>names</em>, odd Host/SNI pairs, and rare spoofed forwarding headers.
    See <strong>Probe behaviour</strong> below.
  </div>

  <h2>Did they know the name?</h2>
  <p>
    We classify each request from the HTTP <code>Host</code> header and TLS SNI
    (<code>tls_server_name</code>):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>named</strong> — <code>ipthing.net</code> (or a subdomain) appears in Host and/or SNI. Strong signal the client resolved or was given the DNS name.</li>
    <li><strong>raw IP</strong> — Host is a literal IPv4 address (often <code>159.203.159.64</code> or an older droplet IP). Typical of address-space scanners and tools that never looked up the name.</li>
    <li><strong>other Host</strong> — some other hostname (CDN leftovers, random/spoofed names). Often opportunistic TLS or misdirected probes.</li>
    <li><strong>unknown</strong> — Host was empty; common on older rows before that field was logged.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.access | safe }}</div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Access mode</th><th>Requests</th><th>Distinct IPs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in access %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.access_mode }}</td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.requests) }}</td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.unique_ips) }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.access_daily | safe }}</div>
  <p>
    Top <code>Host</code> values (including empty) show the same split in raw form —
    named traffic clusters on <code>ipthing.net</code>; raw-IP traffic on the server addresses.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Host header</th><th>Requests</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in top_hosts %}
      <tr><td><code>{{ row.host }}</code></td><td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.requests) }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Probe behaviour</h2>
  <p>
    Security-oriented shape of the traffic: verbs and paths, query-parameter
    names scanners inject, whether clients send spoofed forwarding headers, and
    whether HTTP <code>Host</code> matches TLS SNI. Counts only — no raw header
    dumps or query values. Only <code>GET</code> was accepted until Wednesday
    23&nbsp;September&nbsp;2026; other methods in the charts are from that date
    onward.
  </p>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><div class="label">With query params</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.with_query_params) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Distinct methods</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.distinct_methods) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Distinct paths</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.distinct_paths) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Claimed X-Forwarded-For</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.with_claimed_xff) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">CF-Connecting-IP set</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.with_cf_connecting_ip) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">XFF ≠ TCP peer</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.xff_differs_from_peer) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">CF-IP ≠ TCP peer</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.cf_differs_from_peer) }}</div></div>
    <div class="card"><div class="label">Cookie-bearing</div><div class="value">{{ "{:,}".format(probe.with_cookies) }}</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    ipthing trusts the TCP peer address, not client-supplied forwarding headers.
    Non-empty <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> / <code>CF-Connecting-IP</code> that
    disagree with the peer are classic spoof attempts (or traffic that really
    did pass a proxy). Both are rare here — rarity is the finding.
  </div>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.methods | safe }}</div>
  <div class="note">
    Only <code>GET</code> was allowed until Wednesday 23&nbsp;September&nbsp;2026.
    Earlier rows cannot show other verbs even if clients tried them.
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Requests</th><th>Distinct IPs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in methods %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ row.method }}</code></td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.requests) }}</td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.unique_ips) }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.paths | safe }}</div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Requests</th><th>Distinct IPs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in paths %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ row.path }}</code></td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.requests) }}</td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.unique_ips) }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Query parameter <em>names</em> (not values) are a cheap fingerprint of
    vulnerability scanners: <code>phpinfo</code>, <code>rest_route</code>,
    <code>file</code>, Xdebug session starters, and similar show up even when
    the path is still <code>/</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.query_keys | safe }}</div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Query param name</th><th>Requests</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in query_keys %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ row.param_key }}</code></td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.requests) }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Host vs SNI: agreement usually means a normal TLS client. Empty SNI with a
    Host set is common on plain HTTP or incomplete older rows. A mismatch
    (when both are set) is a stronger opportunistic / misdirected probe signal.
  </p>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.host_sni | safe }}</div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Host / SNI relation</th><th>Requests</th><th>Distinct IPs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in host_sni %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.host_sni_relation }}</td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.requests) }}</td>
        <td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.unique_ips) }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Volume over time</h2>
  <p>
    Spikes often align with internet-wide scanning and cloud automation, not only
    human “what’s my IP?” visits. Quiet days and noisy days are both informative.
  </p>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.daily | safe }}</div>

  <h2>Where clients appear to be</h2>
  <p>
    Country and org come from a geolocation cache (ipinfo-style fields) keyed by IP.
    Cloud and CDN ASNs dominate because scanners, health checks, and hosted tools
    live there — not because “most humans” do.
  </p>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.countries | safe }}</div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Distinct IPs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in countries %}
      <tr><td>{{ row.country }}</td><td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.ips) }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Which networks show up most</h2>
  <p>
    High ranks for DigitalOcean, Google, Cloudflare, Amazon, Microsoft, and
    Censys are expected for a reachable reflector: research scanners, bots, and
    cloud VMs check such hosts continuously.
  </p>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.orgs | safe }}</div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Org / ASN label</th><th>Distinct IPs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for row in orgs %}
      <tr><td>{{ row.org }}</td><td>{{ "{:,}".format(row.ips) }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>TLS and HTTP versions</h2>
  <p>
    Modern stacks prefer TLS&nbsp;1.3 and HTTP/2. Older protocols still appear
    from legacy clients and some scanners. Empty protocol fields usually mean
    older log rows before those columns were populated.
  </p>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.tls | safe }}</div>
  <div class="chart">{{ charts.proto | safe }}</div>

  <h2>Client flavour (User-Agent heuristics)</h2>
  <p>
    Buckets are coarse string heuristics, not a security product. They separate
    casual browser-like traffic from curl/scripts and explicit bot UAs so the
    report can talk about mix without claiming perfect classification.
  </p>
  <div class="chart chart-desktop-only">{{ charts.ua | safe }}</div>
  <div class="chart chart-mobile-only">{{ charts.ua_mobile | safe }}</div>

  <h2>How to read this responsibly</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Selection bias: only hosts that chose (or were pointed at) ipthing.</li>
    <li>Geo/ASN labels are best-effort and can lag or mis-attribute shared IPs.</li>
    <li>Public pages should stay aggregated; raw headers, cookies, and query <em>values</em> are out of scope here (param <em>names</em> only).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="meta">
    Built from the <code>ipthing</code> Neon database by the
    <code>ipthing-analysis</code> report tool. Live at
    <a href="https://stats.ipthing.net/">stats.ipthing.net</a>.
    Source service:
    <a href="https://github.com/jonhadfield/ipthing">jonhadfield/ipthing</a>.
  </p>
</main>

<script>
(function () {
  function resizePlots() {
    if (!window.Plotly) return;
    document.querySelectorAll(".js-plotly-plot").forEach(function (el) {
      try { Plotly.Plots.resize(el); } catch (e) {}
    });
  }
  window.addEventListener("resize", resizePlots);
  window.addEventListener("orientationchange", function () {
    setTimeout(resizePlots, 150);
  });
  if (document.fonts && document.fonts.ready) {
    document.fonts.ready.then(resizePlots);
  }
  setTimeout(resizePlots, 50);
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
